# DuDuClaw OS — Entry B: automatic, tamper-evident boot audit marker.
#
# Runs BEFORE the rescue shell exists at all (Before=duduclaw-rescue-
# shell@.service) and is NOT dependent on the operator cooperating — the
# whole point is that entering rescue mode itself leaves a trace even if
# the person at the console never types anything (commercial/docs/DESIGN-
# maintenance-mode-2026-08.md §3.4: "即使拿到機器的人在當下不受即時稽核
# ...進入 rescue 環境本身也要留痕").
#
# WS-3/B3 (2026-09-01, DESIGN-os-security-line-2026-09.md §2 支柱二 B3):
# the "/data does not exist yet" honest limit this comment originally
# recorded (2026-08-26, Y7-2) no longer holds — /data landed on this line
# via Y8-1/Y9-1 (files/wic/duduclaw-ab-bootdisk.wks.in p4, present on
# every A/B-layout image including duduclaw-image-appliance, the shipping
# target). duduclaw-rescue-audit.sh now writes to
# /data/duduclaw/audit/rescue/ (durable across an A/B slot switch, unlike
# root, which the update mechanism replaces wholesale), with a same-script
# fallback to the old /var/log/duduclaw/ path if /data turns out to be
# unavailable — see the script's own header for why the fallback exists
# and is not merely theoretical.
#
# RequiresMountsFor=/data (new): makes the dependency on /data being
# mounted EXPLICIT rather than relying purely on the implicit
# basic.target→sysinit.target→local-fs.target ordering chain
# duduclaw-rescue.target's own (DefaultDependencies-on) Requires=
# basic.target already provides — checked, not assumed: /data's own
# fstab entry (wks p4, `--use-uuid`, no `noauto`) generates a normal
# data.mount unit pulled into local-fs.target by default, which THIS
# target already implicitly waits for before activating (confirmed by
# duduclaw-rescue-root-lock.service's own pre-existing `After=
# local-fs.target`, same target, same implicit chain). RequiresMountsFor=
# adds systemd's own explicit wait-for-this-specific-mount semantics on
# top of that inference — most valuable exactly in the failure case
# rescue mode itself exists for (a disk/boot problem serious enough to
# need Entry B), where /data mounting cleanly should never be assumed. A
# failed RequiresMountsFor= only fails THIS service (Wants=, not
# Requires=, from duduclaw-rescue.target — see that target's own [Unit]
# section), never the rescue target itself or the shell units, so rescue
# mode stays reachable even if /data is genuinely gone; the script's own
# fallback additionally covers the case where /data mounts but is
# read-only/full/otherwise unwritable, which RequiresMountsFor= alone
# would not catch.
#
# ORDERING FINDING (2026-09-01, found while implementing B3, not part of
# B3's own original scope but directly relevant): duduclaw-rescue-root-
# lock.service's own `Before=...duduclaw-rescue-audit.service` means `/`
# is ALREADY remounted read-only by the time this unit's ExecStart runs —
# on the OLD /var/log/duduclaw/ target (root-resident, no /var overlay on
# this line yet per G3/P1), `mkdir -p`/`touch` would hit EROFS and, under
# this script's own `set -e`, fail the ENTIRE unit before a single byte
# was ever written — the audit marker this unit exists to guarantee could
# never actually land. Moving the primary write target to /data (a
# separately-mounted partition, unaffected by root's own read-only
# remount) is not merely a persistence improvement, it is what makes this
# unit succeed at all under the existing, unchanged root-lock ordering —
# not independently re-verified against a real boot in this ticket
# (recipe-only, no bitbake/QEMU run), flagged as a concrete item for the
# next real rescue-mode boot test.
[Unit]
Description=DuDuClaw rescue mode: write boot audit marker
DefaultDependencies=no
Requisite=duduclaw-rescue.target
After=local-fs.target systemd-journald.service
RequiresMountsFor=/data
Before=duduclaw-rescue-shell@.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
RemainAfterExit=yes
ExecStart=/usr/sbin/duduclaw-rescue-audit.sh
